how fast the universe is growing has  been a question astronomers have been  trying to answer since the late 1920s by  the 1990s data from the Hubble Space  Telescope estimated the speed of the  expansion of the universe a number  called the Hubble constant to somewhere  in the 70 kilometers per second per  megaparsec range in 2011 the best data  put it close to seventy four kilometers  per second per megaparsec it seemed as  if the Astronomy community was finally  settling on a number then in 2013 data  from the Planck satellite suggested a  lower number for the Hubble constant  planks measurements indicated that the  universe was expanding at a rate of  sixty seven point three kilometers per  second per megaparsec that doesn't seem  like a big difference but it is planck's  value is the best fit for a whole model  of the universe including one for the  nature of dark energy so the discrepancy  could mean that astronomers recipe for  what makes up the universe is a little  bit off the universe could have a little  more dark matter an ordinary matter and  a little less dark energy than  previously predicted despite what the  plank data suggests astronomers aren't  quite ready to overhaul their  understanding of the universe scientists  say they need more data which they'll  get from satellites such as Gaia maybe  then they'll have a better idea about  what makes up the universe and what its  ultimate fate will be
 

